    {
     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\":4,\"rationale\":\"Without blocking appServer:lateralMovement, it stays reachable, because memoryDump:credentialAccess is reachable straight from the entry footholds. appServer then feeds vaultRead, codeRepo and dbReplica directly, so all three would have to be blocked. On top of that, shareMount stays reachable through jumpHost:lateralMovement, which is fed from configSecret:credentialAccess (from supplychain). That forces a fourth block. Blocking appServer's three parents instead (memoryDump, apiToken, ssoLogin) costs 3 and still leaves the jumpHost route open, so that is also at least 4. If appServer is blocked, three independent routes remain, and they share no blockable step: (a) jumpHost leads to shareMount; (b) memoryDump leads to printServer and on to dbReplica; (c) ssoLogin plus vpnSession lead to serviceAccount and on to codeRepo. Each needs its own block, again totalling 4. Any 3-step set therefore fails. A working set of 4 is {appServer, jumpHost, printServer, serviceAccount}; blocking the four collection steps also works.\"}"
    }
   ],
